</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Among static drafters, EAGLE-3 reaches the highest <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{=}5.89" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5.89</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{=}5.89</annotation></semantics></math> through seven sequential drafter calls but pays a <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="31\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>31</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">31\%</annotation></semantics></math> drafter share, while parallel drafters such as Medusa and ParallelSpec cut this to <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9\%</annotation></semantics></math> at the cost of capping <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> at around <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>. SpecBlock condenses drafting into two block forwards, dropping drafter time from <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>17</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17</annotation></semantics></math> ms to <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math> ms while the layer-wise shift retains <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{=}4.41" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4.41</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{=}4.41</annotation></semantics></math> on Llama-3.1-8B, only <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.48" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.48</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.48</annotation></semantics></math> tokens below EAGLE-3 despite the much shorter chain. Cost-aware adapt and SpecBlock+OSD reach essentially the same <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, within <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.02</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.02</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, but SpecBlock+adapt holds <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathcal{D}}\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></msub><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathcal{D}}\%</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>13</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>18</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18</annotation></semantics></math> versus OSD’s <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>14</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>19</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19</annotation></semantics></math>: the bandit skips weak signals and routes most updates to the head-only action, whose backward over the lm_head and rank head is more than an order of magnitude cheaper than a full-drafter backward. At <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{=}0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{=}0</annotation></semantics></math>, single-domain benchmarks such as NQ and MATH-500 lift <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0</annotation></semantics></math> as streaming queries share a stable target, while mixed-instruction Alpaca lifts only <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.05</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.07" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.07</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.07</annotation></semantics></math> because gradients across instruction types partially cancel.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The trade-off strengthens on larger targets: drafter share falls from <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="31\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>31</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">31\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>24</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24\%</annotation></semantics></math> for EAGLE-3 between Llama-3.1-8B and Qwen3-32B, and from <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11\%</annotation></semantics></math> for SpecBlock, narrowing SpecBlock’s relative drafting cost from <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="52\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>52</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">52\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="46\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>46</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">46\%</annotation></semantics></math> of EAGLE-3’s. Sampling at <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{=}1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{=}1.0</annotation></semantics></math> reduces <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> across all methods because rejection sampling against a high-entropy target accepts fewer draft tokens, but the relative ordering is unchanged.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Ablations</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate each design component, Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.3 Ablations ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ SpecBlock: Block-Iterative Speculative Decoding with Dynamic Tree Drafting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> removes one component per row on Llama-3.1-8B at <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{=}0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{=}0</annotation></semantics></math>, with the base group on the six benchmarks and the adaptation group on the four where the bandit engages. Base rows ablate the prefix broadcast, the layer-wise shift, the valid-prefix curriculum, and the rank-guided branching, with the last replaced by a uniform fixed-<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> tree at the same node budget. Adaptation rows ablate the cost-aware bandit, leaving an always-update policy, and the head-only action, forcing every triggered update to a full-drafter backward.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Ablations on Llama-3.1-8B at <math id="S4.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{=}0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{=}0</annotation></semantics></math>, removing one component of the base architecture (top) or of cost-aware serving-time adaptation (bottom) per row.</figcaption>
